data: {"type":"response.reasoning_text.done","item_id":"rs_resp_91fcfea092ec447bb4cf8f60a144103b","output_index":0,"text":"\nWe need n divisible by 12, exactly 12 positive divisors, smallest such n.\n\nn divisible by 12 = 2²·3. So n = 2^a · 3^b · ... with a ≥ 2, b ≥ 1.\n\nDivisor count 12: possible forms:\n- p^11\n- p^5 q\n- p^3 q^2\n- p^2 q r\n\nSince n must have 2 and 3 as factors with exponents ≥ 2 and ≥ 1:\n\nForm p^2 q r: n = 2^2 · 3 · r = 4·3·r = 12r where r prime > 3. Smallest r = 5: n = 60. Check: 60 = 2^2·3·5, divisors = 3·2·2 = 12. Yes! 60 divisible by 12? 60/12 = 5. Yes.\n\nForm p^3 q^2: 2^3·3^2 = 72, divisors = 4·3=12. 72 > 60.\n\nForm p^5 q: 2^5·3 = 96 or 3^5·2 = 486 but need 2^2 dividing; 2^5·3 = 96. Larger.\n\nSo answer 60.\n","sequence_number":103,"content_index":0}

